1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,831
It is nine in the morning. A developer fixes a bug, and ships the change.

2
00:00:05,381 --> 00:00:06,275
In one company,

3
00:00:06,275 --> 00:00:08,184
she hears back in eight minutes.

4
00:00:08,184 --> 00:00:09,317
In another company,

5
00:00:09,317 --> 00:00:10,391
with the same bug,

6
00:00:10,391 --> 00:00:11,464
and the same code,

7
00:00:11,464 --> 00:00:13,850
she hears back in five and a half hours.

8
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:19,200
The difference is not talent, and it is not a tool. It is how the people work together.

9
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:23,536
This is lesson one of DevOps fundamentals: the principles.

10
00:00:23,536 --> 00:00:24,434
Collaboration,

11
00:00:24,434 --> 00:00:25,011
feedback,

12
00:00:25,011 --> 00:00:26,359
and shared ownership.

13
00:00:26,359 --> 00:00:28,219
And a lab that measures them.

14
00:00:29,769 --> 00:00:31,198
Here is what to expect.

15
00:00:31,748 --> 00:00:35,823
One: the wall between development and operations, and why it hurts.

16
00:00:36,373 --> 00:00:40,981
Two: the core principles: collaboration, fast feedback, and shared ownership.

17
00:00:41,531 --> 00:00:44,923
Three: three feedback loops, and what slows each one down.

18
00:00:45,473 --> 00:00:46,625
Four: a live demo,

19
00:00:46,625 --> 00:00:49,825
where we measure both worlds with a real terminal,

20
00:00:49,825 --> 00:00:51,233
and fix who owns what.

21
00:00:51,783 --> 00:00:54,962
This is lesson one of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

22
00:00:57,012 --> 00:00:58,804
Start with the old way of working.

23
00:00:59,354 --> 00:01:03,706
Developers are rewarded for change. They want to ship new features, fast.

24
00:01:04,256 --> 00:01:06,551
Operations are rewarded for stability.

25
00:01:06,551 --> 00:01:08,243
They want nothing to change,

26
00:01:08,243 --> 00:01:09,995
because change breaks things.

27
00:01:10,545 --> 00:01:12,301
So there is a wall between them.

28
00:01:12,301 --> 00:01:13,563
The developer finishes,

29
00:01:13,563 --> 00:01:15,430
and throws the code over the wall,

30
00:01:15,430 --> 00:01:16,198
with a ticket.

31
00:01:16,748 --> 00:01:18,052
Operations catch it,

32
00:01:18,052 --> 00:01:18,834
a day later,

33
00:01:18,834 --> 00:01:19,878
with no context.

34
00:01:19,878 --> 00:01:20,464
It fails,

35
00:01:20,464 --> 00:01:22,551
and they throw the problem back.

36
00:01:23,101 --> 00:01:24,834
Each side is doing its job.

37
00:01:24,834 --> 00:01:26,310
And the customer waits.

38
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:28,364
Both teams have different goals,

39
00:01:28,364 --> 00:01:30,482
and nobody owns the whole result.

40
00:01:31,032 --> 00:01:33,264
Dev ops is the answer to that wall.

41
00:01:33,264 --> 00:01:34,730
It is a way of working,

42
00:01:34,730 --> 00:01:35,431
not a tool,

43
00:01:35,431 --> 00:01:36,707
and not a job title.

44
00:01:38,257 --> 00:01:40,049
There are four ideas to remember.

45
00:01:40,599 --> 00:01:41,943
One: collaboration.

46
00:01:41,943 --> 00:01:42,721
Developers,

47
00:01:42,721 --> 00:01:43,500
operations,

48
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:44,066
testers,

49
00:01:44,066 --> 00:01:46,188
and security work as one team,

50
00:01:46,188 --> 00:01:48,664
towards one goal: working software,

51
00:01:48,664 --> 00:01:50,220
in the hands of users.

52
00:01:50,770 --> 00:01:51,873
Two: fast feedback.

53
00:01:51,873 --> 00:01:54,485
The sooner you learn that something is wrong,

54
00:01:54,485 --> 00:01:55,935
the cheaper it is to fix.

55
00:01:55,935 --> 00:01:57,618
Minutes are better than days.

56
00:01:58,168 --> 00:01:59,662
Three: shared ownership.

57
00:01:59,662 --> 00:02:00,471
You build it,

58
00:02:00,471 --> 00:02:01,155
you run it.

59
00:02:01,155 --> 00:02:04,204
The team that writes a service is on-call for it.

60
00:02:04,204 --> 00:02:04,702
No more,

61
00:02:04,702 --> 00:02:05,635
not my problem.

62
00:02:06,185 --> 00:02:11,412
Four: continuous improvement. Measure, learn, and make a small change. Then do it again.

63
00:02:11,962 --> 00:02:13,667
Automation helps all four.

64
00:02:13,667 --> 00:02:18,063
But automation without these ideas only makes a bad process faster.

65
00:02:19,613 --> 00:02:22,322
Feedback comes in loops. Three of them matter most.

66
00:02:22,872 --> 00:02:26,609
The fastest loop is the developer's own: run the unit tests,

67
00:02:26,609 --> 00:02:27,294
in seconds.

68
00:02:27,294 --> 00:02:28,290
If this is slow,

69
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:29,784
everything else is slow.

70
00:02:30,334 --> 00:02:32,726
The next loop is the pipeline: build,

71
00:02:32,726 --> 00:02:33,049
test,

72
00:02:33,049 --> 00:02:34,795
and scan after each commit,

73
00:02:34,795 --> 00:02:35,506
in minutes.

74
00:02:35,506 --> 00:02:38,932
A broken change is rejected before it reaches anyone.

75
00:02:39,482 --> 00:02:42,242
The longest loop is production: monitoring,

76
00:02:42,242 --> 00:02:42,691
alerts,

77
00:02:42,691 --> 00:02:43,333
and users.

78
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:45,900
It tells you if the change really works,

79
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:46,478
in hours,

80
00:02:46,478 --> 00:02:46,991
or days.

81
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:49,847
A healthy team shortens every loop.

82
00:02:49,847 --> 00:02:51,428
And when a loop is slow,

83
00:02:51,428 --> 00:02:53,998
it asks one question: what is the wait?

84
00:02:53,998 --> 00:02:54,986
Is it a person,

85
00:02:54,986 --> 00:02:55,579
a ticket,

86
00:02:55,579 --> 00:02:56,106
a queue,

87
00:02:56,106 --> 00:02:57,226
or a manual step?

88
00:02:58,776 --> 00:03:03,747
Now ownership. A service with no clear owner is a service that nobody fixes.

89
00:03:04,297 --> 00:03:06,205
The rule is simple: you build it,

90
00:03:06,205 --> 00:03:06,842
you run it.

91
00:03:06,842 --> 00:03:09,907
The team that builds a service owns it in production.

92
00:03:10,457 --> 00:03:15,321
Each service has one owning team, and one person on-call, who comes from that team.

93
00:03:15,871 --> 00:03:17,165
When something breaks,

94
00:03:17,165 --> 00:03:18,810
the team asks what happened,

95
00:03:18,810 --> 00:03:20,574
and how the system allowed it.

96
00:03:20,574 --> 00:03:21,750
Not who is to blame.

97
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:24,042
That is called a blameless post mortem.

98
00:03:24,592 --> 00:03:29,157
Blame makes people hide problems. A blameless review makes the system better.

99
00:03:30,707 --> 00:03:32,008
Three myths to avoid.

100
00:03:32,558 --> 00:03:34,312
Myth one: DevOps is a tool.

101
00:03:34,312 --> 00:03:36,505
No tool gives you shared ownership.

102
00:03:36,505 --> 00:03:37,883
A tool can support it,

103
00:03:37,883 --> 00:03:39,449
and it cannot replace it.

104
00:03:39,999 --> 00:03:41,811
Myth two: DevOps is a team.

105
00:03:41,811 --> 00:03:43,040
A new DevOps team,

106
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:44,270
between the others,

107
00:03:44,270 --> 00:03:45,823
can become another wall.

108
00:03:46,373 --> 00:03:49,156
Myth three: DevOps is only about speed.

109
00:03:49,156 --> 00:03:51,869
Speed without stability is not DevOps.

110
00:03:51,869 --> 00:03:53,470
The goal is both: fast,

111
00:03:53,470 --> 00:03:54,096
and safe.

112
00:03:55,646 --> 00:03:59,870
Go to the demo folder.

113
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:03,319
Look around.

114
00:04:03,869 --> 00:04:04,814
Two event logs,

115
00:04:04,814 --> 00:04:06,579
one for each way of working.

116
00:04:06,579 --> 00:04:07,777
A list of services,

117
00:04:07,777 --> 00:04:09,479
and a list of team members.

118
00:04:10,029 --> 00:04:13,272
The wall.

119
00:04:13,822 --> 00:04:14,856
A commit at nine.

120
00:04:14,856 --> 00:04:16,316
A ticket for operations.

121
00:04:16,316 --> 00:04:18,809
They start the build at twenty to twelve,

122
00:04:18,809 --> 00:04:19,600
and it fails.

123
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:22,398
The developer hears about it at half past two.

124
00:04:22,948 --> 00:04:26,575
Shared ownership.

125
00:04:27,125 --> 00:04:28,183
A commit at nine.

126
00:04:28,183 --> 00:04:30,674
The pipeline builds at two minutes past,

127
00:04:30,674 --> 00:04:33,102
and the tests fail at six minutes past.

128
00:04:33,102 --> 00:04:35,717
The developer is told in the pull request,

129
00:04:35,717 --> 00:04:37,087
at eight minutes past.

130
00:04:37,637 --> 00:04:44,763
Measure it.

131
00:04:53,413 --> 00:04:56,527
awk turns each time into minutes. Then it prints the gap.

132
00:04:57,077 --> 00:05:02,048
The wall.

133
00:05:02,598 --> 00:05:05,457
Three hundred thirty minutes. Five and a half hours.

134
00:05:06,007 --> 00:05:07,436
Shared.

135
00:05:09,686 --> 00:05:10,489
Eight minutes.

136
00:05:10,489 --> 00:05:11,235
The same bug,

137
00:05:11,235 --> 00:05:13,702
and three hundred twenty-two minutes saved.

138
00:05:13,702 --> 00:05:15,595
That is what fast feedback means.

139
00:05:16,145 --> 00:05:19,729
Who owns what?

140
00:05:20,279 --> 00:05:22,684
Every service is owned by ops hand off,

141
00:05:22,684 --> 00:05:24,040
and nobody is on-call.

142
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:25,705
If the cart breaks tonight,

143
00:05:25,705 --> 00:05:26,445
who answers?

144
00:05:26,995 --> 00:05:35,891
Fix it.

145
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:42,755
Cart is owned by cart,

146
00:05:42,755 --> 00:05:43,995
with Maya on-call.

147
00:05:43,995 --> 00:05:45,854
Search is owned by catalog,

148
00:05:45,854 --> 00:05:46,543
with Lena.

149
00:05:46,543 --> 00:05:48,264
Pay is owned by payments,

150
00:05:48,264 --> 00:05:48,884
with Raj.

151
00:05:48,884 --> 00:05:50,605
Email is owned by growth,

152
00:05:50,605 --> 00:05:51,225
with Zoe.

153
00:05:51,775 --> 00:05:54,825
Write what you learned.

154
00:05:58,575 --> 00:06:02,287
The shared way is shorter, and it saves three hundred twenty-two minutes.

155
00:06:02,837 --> 00:06:06,101
Check the file.

156
00:06:06,651 --> 00:06:07,669
Shared is shorter,

157
00:06:07,669 --> 00:06:10,269
and it saves three hundred twenty-two minutes.

158
00:06:10,269 --> 00:06:12,530
Those are numbers you measured yourself,

159
00:06:12,530 --> 00:06:13,265
not opinions.

160
00:06:14,815 --> 00:06:19,572
Your practice task: measure feedback, and fix ownership. Use the demo files.

161
00:06:20,122 --> 00:06:21,743
One: write the feedback script.

162
00:06:21,743 --> 00:06:25,456
It prints the minutes from the first commit to the first feedback line.

163
00:06:26,006 --> 00:06:27,431
Two: run it on both logs.

164
00:06:27,431 --> 00:06:29,483
You should get three hundred thirty,

165
00:06:29,483 --> 00:06:30,052
and eight.

166
00:06:30,052 --> 00:06:31,306
Then try your own log,

167
00:06:31,306 --> 00:06:33,472
and check that the script still works.

168
00:06:34,022 --> 00:06:35,975
Three: edit the services file.

169
00:06:35,975 --> 00:06:38,514
Every service is owned by its own team,

170
00:06:38,514 --> 00:06:40,272
and has one person on-call,

171
00:06:40,272 --> 00:06:41,574
from that same team.

172
00:06:42,124 --> 00:06:46,668
Four: write the principles file: which way is shorter, and how many minutes it saves.

173
00:06:48,718 --> 00:06:51,939
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

174
00:06:52,489 --> 00:06:58,164
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

175
00:06:58,714 --> 00:07:02,981
Two: the folder academy/lesson145-demo.

176
00:07:03,531 --> 00:07:05,992
Three: four files: wall.log,

177
00:07:05,992 --> 00:07:07,145
shared.log,

178
00:07:07,145 --> 00:07:08,607
services.csv,

179
00:07:08,607 --> 00:07:10,299
and members.csv.

180
00:07:10,299 --> 00:07:12,683
And a read me with the answers.

181
00:07:13,233 --> 00:07:14,937
Four: clone the repository,

182
00:07:14,937 --> 00:07:16,199
and copy the folder.

183
00:07:16,199 --> 00:07:18,092
You need only a shell and awk.

184
00:07:18,092 --> 00:07:19,291
Nothing to install.

185
00:07:19,841 --> 00:07:21,692
Five: this domain has no lab.

186
00:07:21,692 --> 00:07:23,735
It is about ideas and decisions,

187
00:07:23,735 --> 00:07:25,331
so the practice is plain,

188
00:07:25,331 --> 00:07:27,820
and the answers are on the next screen.

189
00:07:28,370 --> 00:07:32,999
Labs come back in the next domains, wherever there is something to build, and to break.

190
00:07:35,049 --> 00:07:37,524
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

191
00:07:38,074 --> 00:07:41,316
One: the script gives three hundred thirty, for the wall log.

192
00:07:41,866 --> 00:07:43,808
Two: and eight, for the shared log.

193
00:07:44,358 --> 00:07:47,003
Three: it still works on a log you wrote yourself.

194
00:07:47,553 --> 00:07:50,540
Four: every service is owned by the team that builds it.

195
00:07:51,090 --> 00:07:54,823
Five: every service has an on-call person, from its own team.

196
00:07:55,373 --> 00:07:59,213
Six: the principles file says shared, and three hundred twenty-two.

197
00:07:59,763 --> 00:08:02,003
If all six are ticked, you have the idea.

198
00:08:04,053 --> 00:08:05,824
Your turn, on your own machine.

199
00:08:06,374 --> 00:08:09,275
One: copy the demo folder, and read the two logs.

200
00:08:09,825 --> 00:08:12,598
Two: write the feedback script, and run it on both.

201
00:08:13,148 --> 00:08:15,751
Three: fix the owners, and the on-call people.

202
00:08:16,301 --> 00:08:18,114
Four: write the principles file.

203
00:08:18,664 --> 00:08:23,614
Five: tick the six points. Then think about your own team: where is your longest wait?

204
00:08:24,164 --> 00:08:25,998
Pause the video, and begin now.

205
00:08:41,548 --> 00:08:42,423
Quick check.

206
00:08:42,973 --> 00:08:44,679
What problem does the wall cause?

207
00:08:45,229 --> 00:08:46,680
Name the four principles.

208
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:49,129
What does you build it, you run it mean?

209
00:08:49,679 --> 00:08:51,513
Why is a blameless review better?

210
00:08:52,063 --> 00:08:54,248
Objective covered: MC16,

211
00:08:54,248 --> 00:08:56,643
objective one: DevOps principles,

212
00:08:56,643 --> 00:08:57,630
collaboration,

213
00:08:57,630 --> 00:08:58,264
feedback,

214
00:08:58,264 --> 00:08:59,743
and shared ownership.

215
